# Afiliantka Project Guidelines

## Product Documentation

Pełna specyfikacja produktu (wizja, aktualne i planowane funkcje, przepływy biznesowe, model danych): [`docs/PROJECT_SPEC.md`](../../docs/PROJECT_SPEC.md). Przeczytaj ją przed implementacją nowych funkcji biznesowych.

## Architecture

This is a dual-purpose Next.js 15 app (App Router) with two hosts:
- **Public website** (`WEB_HOST`) — Polish-language affiliate offers landing page, route group `app/(website)/`
- **App platform** (`APP_HOST`) — authenticated dashboard/admin, route group `app/(app)/`

Routing between hosts is handled by subdomain-based middleware in `middleware.ts`.

## Tech Stack

- **Framework**: Next.js 15 (App Router, Turbopack)
- **CMS**: Sanity — schemas defined inline in `sanity.config.ts`, client at `sanity/lib/client.ts`
- **Auth**: Supabase (magic link OTP) — server client at `lib/supabase/server.ts`, browser client at `lib/supabase/client.ts`
- **File storage**: Vercel Blob (small files/uploads) + Google Drive API via service account (large content)
- **Database**: Supabase (roles, user_roles, drive_sources tables)
- **UI**: Tailwind CSS 4, shadcn/ui components in `components/ui/`, Lucide icons
- **Language**: TypeScript strict, Polish UI text

## Key Conventions

### Route Groups
- `app/(website)/` — public pages, no auth required
- `app/(app)/` — authenticated app: dashboard, admin, studio, auth, API routes
- Never create routes outside these two groups (except `app/layout.tsx` and `app/globals.css`)

### API Routes
- All API routes live under `app/(app)/api/`
- Every API route must check Supabase session: `const { data: { session } } = await supabase.auth.getSession()`
- Admin-only routes must additionally call `isCurrentUserAdmin()` from `lib/roles.ts`
- Return `{ message: "..." }` in error responses with appropriate HTTP status

### Auth & Roles
- Auth uses Supabase magic link OTP — no passwords
- Roles stored in `roles` + `user_roles` tables, checked via RPC `is_user_admin`
- Use `createClient()` for user-scoped operations, `createAdminClient()` for service-role operations
- Admin check: `isCurrentUserAdmin()` from `lib/roles.ts`

### Sanity CMS
- Single client: `import { client } from "@/sanity/lib/client"` — do NOT create duplicate clients
- Schemas: `offer`, `heroSection`, `news` (defined in `sanity.config.ts`)
- Images: `urlFor()` from `lib/sanity-image.ts`
- Files: `getFileUrl()` from `lib/sanity-file.ts`
- Portable Text blocks must be filtered before rendering: `blocks.filter(b => b && b._type)`

### Google Drive
- Service account client at `lib/google-drive.ts`
- Drive sources (which folders/files to expose) stored in `drive_sources` Supabase table
- Admin manages sources via `/admin/drive` (browse picker UI)
- Downloads proxied through `/api/drive/download` which validates access against `drive_sources`
- Always handle `null`/`undefined` mimeType — default to `"application/octet-stream"`

### File Handling
- Vercel Blob: upload via `/api/blobs`, list via `/api/blobs/list`
- Google Drive: list via `/api/drive/list`, download via `/api/drive/download`
- Shared types in `types/file.ts` — `FileItem` normalizes both sources
- Dashboard pages fetch both sources in parallel and merge results

### Components
- UI primitives: `components/ui/` (shadcn/ui — button, card, input, badge, table, skeleton, scroll-area)
- Layout components: `components/layout/` (header)
- Section components: `components/sections/` (hero, featured-offers, all-offers-table)
- Dashboard sidebar: `components/dashboard/sidebar.tsx`
- Admin sidebar: `components/admin/sidebar.tsx`
- All new components MUST follow mobile-first responsive patterns (see UI / Responsive Design below)

## Code Style

- No `console.log` — use `console.error` only in catch blocks for real errors
- No `any` type — use proper types or `unknown` with type guards
- No unused imports or variables
- Polish language for all user-facing text
- No comments that just narrate what code does — only explain non-obvious intent
- Always guard against `null`/`undefined` from external APIs before property access
- Use `"use client"` only when the component needs browser APIs or interactivity

## UI / Responsive Design

**Mobile-first is mandatory.** Every page and component must be designed for the smallest viewport first, then enhanced for larger screens with Tailwind breakpoint modifiers.

### Core Rules

- **Start at 320px** — write base styles for mobile, then add `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:` modifiers for larger screens
- **No fixed widths on containers** — use `w-full`, `max-w-*`, or percentage-based sizing; never hardcode pixel widths on layout elements
- **Touch targets** — all interactive elements (buttons, links, checkboxes, toggles) must be at least 44x44px on mobile (`min-h-[44px] min-w-[44px]` or equivalent padding)
- **Typography** — use Tailwind's relative type scale (`text-sm`, `text-base`, `text-lg`); ensure body text is at least `text-sm` (14px) on mobile for readability

### Layout Patterns

- **Sidebar**: must be hidden on mobile with a hamburger toggle or replaced by bottom navigation; visible as a fixed sidebar on `md:` and above
- **Grids**: use `grid-cols-1` as the base, scale up via `sm:grid-cols-2`, `md:grid-cols-3`, `lg:grid-cols-4`
- **Flex layouts**: default to `flex-col` (stacked) on mobile, switch to `flex-row` at `sm:` or `md:`
- **Forms**: inputs and buttons must be `w-full` on mobile, can be constrained on larger screens
- **Tables**: must be horizontally scrollable (`overflow-x-auto`) on mobile, or restructured as stacked cards
- **Modals/Drawers**: full-screen on mobile (`fixed inset-0`), centered overlay on desktop

### Anti-Patterns (NEVER do these)

- Do NOT use `hidden` on an element without a responsive `sm:block`/`md:flex` counterpart — this hides content permanently on mobile
- Do NOT set static `w-64`, `w-80` etc. on layout containers — these break on narrow screens
- Do NOT build desktop-only layouts and add mobile later — always start mobile-first
- Do NOT use small tap targets — icon-only buttons must have padding to reach 44px touch area

### Viewport Verification

Before finalizing any UI change, mentally verify the layout works at these widths:
- **320px** — small phone (iPhone SE)
- **768px** — tablet
- **1280px** — desktop
